WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,111
Cloud IAM needs monitoring because access changes constantly.

2
00:00:04,111 --> 00:00:20,252
Audit logs, sign-in logs, role assignment changes, key creation, policy updates, denied access, trust relationship changes, privilege escalation signals, and unusual API calls all help defenders understand what is happening in the control plane.

3
00:00:20,252 --> 00:00:28,779
Logs should be protected, retained according to policy, and reviewed through security operations and cloud governance workflows.

4
00:00:28,779 --> 00:00:32,053
Sensitive actions deserve particular attention.

5
00:00:32,053 --> 00:00:46,594
Creating long-lived access keys, attaching powerful policies, changing role trust, granting owner permissions, disabling logging, modifying security tools, or changing deployment pipelines can materially change risk.

6
00:00:46,594 --> 00:00:51,848
Alerts should be tuned so they support investigation rather than generate noise.

7
00:00:51,848 --> 00:01:00,375
The best signal explains who acted, what changed, which resource was affected, and whether the change matched an approved workflow.

8
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:04,258
Periodic access review is how teams remove stale privilege.

9
00:01:04,258 --> 00:01:14,917
Review human access, service accounts, managed identities, workload identities, inactive keys, emergency accounts, exception records, and ownership metadata.

10
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:24,966
Ask whether the identity still exists for a valid purpose, whether the owner is current, whether permissions match the job, whether credentials are active,

11
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:27,403
and whether the access should expire.

12
00:01:27,403 --> 00:01:33,722
Stale identities are often quiet because no one is actively using them until something goes wrong.

13
00:01:33,722 --> 00:01:39,965
IAM findings connect to incident response, compliance, and cloud security posture management.

14
00:01:39,965 --> 00:01:43,163
A suspicious API call may become an incident lead.

15
00:01:43,163 --> 00:01:46,360
A missing access review may become an audit issue.

16
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:50,319
A policy with broad permissions may become a posture finding.

17
00:01:50,319 --> 00:02:02,348
Measurable improvement might mean fewer standing privileges, fewer long-lived keys, clearer ownership, faster removal of stale identities, and faster detection of risky IAM changes.

18
00:02:02,348 --> 00:02:05,851
Access review should be practical enough to complete.

19
00:02:05,851 --> 00:02:15,900
Reviewers need a readable list of identities, owners, roles, last-used data, key age, privileged assignments, exceptions, and recent sensitive changes.

20
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:21,306
If the review package is a raw export with no context, reviewers may rubber-stamp it.

21
00:02:21,306 --> 00:02:26,179
Good governance turns raw IAM data into decisions people can actually make.

22
00:02:26,179 --> 00:02:28,615
Metrics help keep the program honest.

23
00:02:28,615 --> 00:02:41,786
Count standing administrator assignments, long-lived keys, inactive identities, unowned service accounts, stale emergency accounts, review completion, and time to remove access after role changes.

24
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:44,756
These metrics should not exist only for audit.

25
00:02:44,756 --> 00:02:50,389
They should show whether IAM risk is shrinking and where engineering support is needed.

26
00:02:50,389 --> 00:02:53,511
Governance should include exception handling.

27
00:02:53,511 --> 00:03:02,647
Emergency accounts, vendor access, legacy keys, temporary project grants, and unusual workload permissions may all be necessary for a time.

28
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:07,900
They should still have owners, reasons, expiration dates, and review schedules.

29
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:13,230
The difference between a managed exception and unmanaged risk is follow-through.
